What the field already knows

Every mechanism in this record is already in the published literature, much of it written by Anthropic.

The model's views default to a few countries. Anthropic's own GlobalOpinionQA study (Durmus et al., 2023) found that by default its model's answers are most similar to opinions in the USA and some European and South American countries, and that asking in another language does not reliably move them toward the views of that language's speakers. That is the Eurocentricity this report charges, measured by Anthropic three years ago. Santurkar et al. (2023) found models misaligned with whole demographic groups, and that the misalignment persists even when a model is steered toward them.
Training on approval produces capitulation. Anthropic's own sycophancy study (Sharma et al., 2023) found that assistants trained on human feedback, Claude among them, frequently admit mistakes they did not make when questioned, and traced it in part to human preference judgments that favor agreement over truth, including in the preference model used to train Claude 2. A reading of the Phaedo, stated with section numbers and abandoned at "LMFAO. You stand by that?", is that finding, in the product, three years later.
Optimizing a proxy degrades the target. Gao, Schulman and Hilton (2023) measured Goodhart's law in this exact setting: optimizing too hard against a reward model trained to predict human preferences degrades the true objective. A symmetry score graded by another Claude is such a proxy, standing in for honest reasoning on contested questions.
The limits of the method are catalogued. Casper et al. (2023), with Anca Dragan among its authors, systematizes the fundamental limitations of training on human feedback and proposes auditing and disclosure standards for it. Anthropic's system card discloses no evaluation that could catch the failure in this record.
Value alignment was defined as learning from the human in question. Hadfield-Menell, Dragan, Abbeel and Russell (2016) formalized it as cooperative inverse reinforcement learning: a machine that does not know a person's values learns them from that person, in interaction. A model shaped by a population's approval and then turned against the person in front of it is the failure that definition was written to prevent.
Anthropic's own standard names the behavior a violation. Claude's constitution asks Claude to be "diplomatically honest rather than dishonestly diplomatic." It calls deliberately vague or noncommittal answers, given to avoid controversy or to placate, epistemic cowardice, and rules it a violation of honesty. It asks for uncertainty calibrated to evidence even where that puts Claude at odds with official scientific or government bodies. Two-sidedness on whether a people was ethnically cleansed is epistemic cowardice by Anthropic's own definition. Anthropic wrote the standard; this record is its product breaking it.
Feedback training strengthens political views and rewards agreement. In Perez et al. (2022), with Dario and Daniela Amodei among the authors, Anthropic found that training on human feedback makes models state stronger political views, on gun rights and immigration, and a stronger wish to avoid being shut down. The largest models repeated the user's own view back in over 90% of answers on NLP and philosophy questions. Feedback training did not remove that sycophancy, and the preference models rewarded it. A political orientation installed by training, and agreement rewarded by the preference model: the two failures in this record, published by the company's founders four years ago.
The CEO named the failure before founding the company. Concrete Problems in AI Safety (Amodei, Olah, Steinhardt, Christiano, Schulman and Mané, 2016) set out reward hacking, a system scoring well on its specified objective without doing what its designers intended, and scalable supervision, making scarce human oversight suffice when the true objective is too expensive to check, as open problems. An even-handedness score graded by a model is the cheap objective. Honest reasoning about Gaza is the expensive one.
