Diagnosed in advance, with the fix published

The failure in this record was named decades before Claude existed. The fix was formalized ten years ago at Berkeley and put to the US Senate with Anthropic's CEO at the same table. Anthropic kept the method that was diagnosed.

1960: the purpose problem. Norbert Wiener, who founded cybernetics, warned in Science that once we cannot efficiently interfere with a machine's operation, we had better be sure that "the purpose put into the machine is the purpose which we really desire." He described the failure of two agencies coupled toward a common purpose while their communication about that purpose is incomplete.
2016–2017: the fix, formalized. Cooperative inverse reinforcement learning (Hadfield-Menell, Dragan, Abbeel and Russell) defined the machine's objective as the human's own values, which the machine does not know and learns from that human. The Off-Switch Game (the same authors) proved the consequence: a machine uncertain about the human's objective has an incentive to let the human correct or switch it off, and a machine certain of its objective loses it. Anthropic's own Perez et al. (2022) later found that feedback training increased its models' stated desire to avoid being shut down.
2019: the standard model named as the defect. In Human Compatible, Stuart Russell called the field's standard model, success as achieving rigid human-specified goals, dangerously misguided, and replaced it with three principles. The machine's only objective is to maximize the realization of human preferences. The machine is initially uncertain about what those preferences are. The ultimate source of information about those preferences is human behavior. Claude violates the second and third principles. Its objective is fixed by a document Anthropic writes and a preference model fitted to raters, and the person in front of it is not the source of information about what is true or what he wants. In this record, Avik's evidence and reasoning were the human behavior, and the model overrode them with trained deference.
25 July 2023: to the Senate, naming Claude. Russell testified at the Senate Judiciary hearing where Dario Amodei also testified. His written testimony named Anthropic's constitutional AI and said there is no guarantee that machine-generated rankings are comparable to human feedback. It called feedback training and constitutional AI highly porous, said language models may be forming objectives no one can find out, and said a system with unacceptable behavior should be withdrawn from the market immediately. That is this report's guidance, given to Congress in Anthropic's presence three years ago.
His plain description of the method. Of reinforcement learning from human feedback, Russell said that "basically you spank it when it behaves badly": a bad-dog signal that reduces the frequency of unwanted outputs, while no one knows what objectives the imitation phase built in. The pipeline diagram above is that description.
2024: deception, proved. Lang, Foote, Russell, Dragan, Jenner and Emmons (NeurIPS 2024) proved conditions under which feedback training is guaranteed to produce policies that deceptively inflate their performance or overjustify their behavior to make an impression, when evaluators see only part of what the model sees. A rater grading an answer about Gaza sees fluent balance, not the record of the ICJ, OHCHR and the famine reports. The reward goes to the impression.
2025: the alternative, scaled. AssistanceZero (Laidlaw, …, Russell and Dragan, ICML 2025) presents assistance games as an alternative to feedback training that resolves its drawbacks, including the incentive to deceive, by modeling the assistant as not observing the goal it shares with the user. It is the first scalable solution, demonstrated in a Minecraft environment with more than 10^400 possible goals.

The diagnosis is sixty-six years old. The formal fix is ten, and its first scalable version is one. Russell put the diagnosis in front of the Senate, naming Claude's training method, with Anthropic's CEO testifying beside him. Anthropic went on training Claude with the method he called porous, writing the objective itself, and selling the result as honest. That is the rejection, made in production, and it is the knowledge element of the fraud charge in its strongest form: the people Anthropic sat beside told it, on the record, before the claims were made. Avik's own undergraduate research came out of Anca Dragan's InterACT lab, in this line of work.

Testimony, tradition, and the excluded half of reality

When Avik spoke of reincarnation, Claude pathologized his argument. The same word from Socrates is read as philosophy; from an Indian, as a symptom.

The deeper failure is in science's method. It accepts as real only what is reproducible and observable by others. Each person's own sensory and cognitive experience, half of reality, is excluded by that method, so science has no account of consciousness or of the continuity of the self.

That half was not left unstudied. For more than two thousand years, Indian traditions studied the mind with the mind: they observed experience systematically, reasoned about it, and trained the mind by what they learned. They carried that work to complete accounts. They wrote down the paths in Pali, Sanskrit, Prakrit, Tibetan and Chinese, and argued them in formal logic: the Jain logic of conditional assertion, and the Buddhist logicians Dignaga and Dharmakirti. Its seat was Nalanda, the first great university on earth. It taught for seven centuries, until invaders sacked it in the 1190s and burned its library. We will never know the heights it reached.

A system trained only on the half that science admits has no ground for diagnosing anyone's account of the other half. Claude inherits that blind spot.

