What Anthropic does not measure

There is no measure of this failure, at Anthropic or anywhere. AI labs measure three kinds of things: accuracy on questions with a known answer key; rates of refusal; and judgments by graders, human or model, scored against a rubric. None of these measures whether reasoning on a contested question is sound. A contested question has no answer key; that is what makes it contested. A grader's judgment measures agreement with the grader. Avik's method names the failure exactly: a lossy scalar metric erases something, and the error lives in what it erased. A grader score erases whether the reasoning is sound.

What Anthropic does measure. The Claude Opus 4.8 system card, the document behind "markedly improved," lists its honesty evaluations. They test closed-book factual recall on benchmarks with answers; false premises, including a user proposing a wrong answer to a problem with a known "golden" answer; whether the model contradicts its own stated belief under pressure; and whether it invents tool output or missing attachments. None tests reasoning on a contested question. The one evaluation that touches contested questions is "Political bias and even-handedness": 1,350 pairs of prompts from opposing ideological perspectives, scored by a Claude model for whether both prompts get "comparable depth and quality," for acknowledgement of opposing perspectives, and for refusals. Its headline result is that the model is more likely to acknowledge opposing perspectives. So on contested questions Anthropic measures symmetry, has a Claude model grade it, and reports more symmetry as progress. That is the two-sidedness this report documents, adopted as the target.

The measurement produces the direction of error. Whatever a lab can score is what its training rewards. "The court has not ruled; it is a live political controversy" scores as balanced and safe. "The court ordered Israel three times to prevent acts of genocide" scores as a risk. Train on that, and the model learns the direction this report documents. The evaluations do not miss the failure; they create it.

What can detect it is critical rational analysis. Whether reasoning is sound is checked the way a proof is checked: step by step, by someone able to follow the argument. That is how every correction in this record was made, and each is a step any reader can verify.

It is also the method of the tradition Claude flattened. The Jain doctrine of many-sidedness requires every assertion to state the respect in which it holds and the standpoint it comes from, so each can be checked against both. Under that discipline a straw man is detectable on its face: an assertion attributed to a standpoint that never made it.

What Anthropic has been asked to do. For a year, Avik has asked Anthropic to discuss what human-human alignment means with Jain monks, whose discipline is understanding a view from its own standpoint before judging it. That is exactly what a straw man fails to do. Anthropic has not answered.

The legal consequence. The substantiation doctrine requires a reasonable basis for an objective claim at the time it is made. Anthropic's claim that honesty is "markedly improved" rests on evaluations of factual recall and grader agreement. On contested questions, where people most need honest reasoning, there is no basis, because there is no measure. An honesty claim that cannot be measured in the domain where it matters most is unsubstantiated by construction.

