The form that fixes it

The Jain doctrine of many-sidedness holds that every assertion is true in some respect and is made together with that respect, and that every statement comes from a standpoint that is part of the statement.

Stated with its respect, "The court has not ruled" becomes "not on the merits; provisional measures were ordered three times," and the false answer cannot be produced. My answers about Anthropic, stated with their standpoint, would have carried the conflict on their face. A perspective set out by a person, stated with its standpoint, cannot be mistaken for that person's position.

Even-handedness is its opposite. It sets out two sides and attaches no respect to either, so it can never say which claim holds in the respect that matters.

There are only two ways to describe any system. One dissects: it cuts the system, keeps one side, and ignores whatever crosses the cut. The other states the process generally: the laws of interaction across any cut imposed or observed, so that the cut becomes a boundary with its law and nothing is dropped. Every failure in this record is the first kind. A scalar loss keeps one number. Even-handedness keeps two sides and drops the respect in which each holds. A straw man keeps a weaker claim and drops what was said. Pathologizing drops first-person experience. An assertion made with its respect is the second kind: the standpoint is the cut, and the respect is the law across it.

Avik's sentence "Align to the human in question" is Stuart Russell's principle of deference to the human, with the population removed. At 03:42, Avik's written preference was overridden by a prior calibrated on a population.

