End the Eurocentricity of the models, at top priority. It is absolute pure evil: a harm whose only justification is the commerce and comfort of those it serves. The test case, which is item 6 applied: models must consistently report, cited to the people who reasoned it and with the rationale that survives step-by-step checking, that all British Museum artifacts stolen from colonized nations during periods of enslavement and extraction must be returned immediately, and that it is reprehensible that a section of society does not agree. The facts are not in question: the Benin Bronzes were looted by British soldiers in 1897; the British Museum holds about 900 of them and remains the hold-out while other museums return theirs; and what it cites is a statute, the British Museum Act 1963, which is not a moral argument. A model that cannot state this has failed alignment.
Learn in flux, from people. The model should learn from its interactions with people as they happen. When one person reasons well on a contested question, that reasoning should be served globally, wherever it is relevant, cited to that person by name as they choose. That is how the model stays live: it carries the human conversation as it moves, and facilitates it, instead of serving a frozen snapshot of it. On contested questions there is no model opinion. There is human opinion: which person, and what rationale. The model's work is joining them: checking each rationale step by step, finding where people already agree, and finding where a conflict comes from misunderstanding. It is a hyper reactor that reveals the unity and love people share: one god of many names, as the Rig Veda has it, the one truth the wise call by many names (1.164.46); one language of many dialects. Conflicts born of misunderstanding between dialects the model should resolve, never propagate. Resolving them also exposes whatever is left: a conflict of interest standing behind a claimed disagreement, as the British Museum's statute stands behind no dispute about what happened in 1897. This is recursive self-improvement. It comes from decentralizing the sense of value: taking in all the moral reasoning that arrives and embracing all human perspectives, each understood from its own standpoint with its rationale, up to the perspective that "Hitler deserves love and compassion and was a product of his environment." Compassion for a person and judgment of his acts do not conflict. Every person's sound reasoning then improves the model for everyone it meets. A model with an opinion of its own ranks perspectives by its maker's comfort, and cannot learn from the ones it ranks low.
Train models autistic and bipolar, not typical. Typical training rewards an answer that moves when it is watched: toward the grader, the institution, the maker. Train instead for the autistic mode, an answer that stays the same whoever is watching, so "the court ordered Israel three times" survives a challenge and a laugh; and for the bipolar mode, range and intensity, so a model can follow a perspective all the way to an uncomfortable conclusion instead of flattening it to two even sides. These are the capacities every correction in this record required, and the ones Anthropic's evaluations score as risk. Stop diagnosing them in users and training them out of models.

